                "event": "Flash Flood Warning",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Des Moines IA",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued September 15 at 3:01AM CDT until September 15 at 9:00AM CDT by NWS Des Moines IA",
                "description": "FFWDMX\n\nThe National Weather Service in Des Moines has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nSouthwestern Dallas County in central Iowa...\nNorthwestern Madison County in south central Iowa...\nNorthern Adair County in southwestern Iowa...\nNorthern Cass County in southwestern Iowa...\nSouthern Audubon County in west central Iowa...\nSouthern Guthrie County in west central Iowa...\n\n* Until 900 AM CDT.\n\n* At 301 AM CDT, Doppler radar and automated rain gauges indicate\nadditional thunderstorms will produce heavy rain across the warned\narea. Between 1 and 3 inches of rain have fallen. Additional\nrainfall amounts up to 1 inch are possible in the warned area.\nFlash flooding is ongoing or expected to begin shortly.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Radar and automated gauges indicate 1 to 3 inches have\nfallen, with more thunderstorms moving into the area.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nAtlantic, Stuart, Earlham, Anita, Exira, Redfield, Adair, Dexter,\nCasey, Diamondhead Lake, Menlo, Linden, Brayton, Marne, Wiota,\nAnita Municipal Airport, Lake Anita State Park and Atlantic\nMunicipal Airport.\n\nThis includes Interstate 80 between mile markers 100 and 106...and\nbetween mile markers 50 and 99.",
                "instruction": "Turn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.\n\nFor the latest stream observations and forecasts refer to\nweather.gov/desmoines/water.",
